            "public_requirement": "A transistor-level peak detector must accept pkdinp and pkdinn and produce a strictly increasing differential detector response at detp and detn.",
            "electrical_reason": "Although these elements form an asymmetric NPN pair with collector RC loads, they do not have usable headroom at the required directly driven detector input common mode. In the supplied peak-detector test failure operating point, pkdinp and pkdinn are both 2.092 V while detp=1.04805 V and detn=1.10803 V, with epk=0.99987 V. Therefore both NPN collector-base junctions are substantially forward biased (their collectors are about 1 V below their bases), so the purported detector is deeply saturated rather than operating as a high-frequency active rectifier. The same packet reports a 17.3 K electrothermal rise for Xqpkp and transient abort, making the specified 33.5 GHz detector operation electrically non-credible rather than merely numerically off target."
